## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical detection pipeline for cloud-native DNS tunneling, combining kernel telemetry, DNS-layer analytics, and service-mesh visibility; emphasizes real-world validation and false-positive control.

## Key Concepts
- eBPF DNS egress monitoring
- CoreDNS analytics and entropy-based signals
- Istio telemetry integration and namespace scoping
- Multi-signal alerting and validation
- Synthetic traffic to verify detections

## Code Example
```javascript
function entropy(s){
  const counts = {};
  for(const ch of s) counts[ch]=(counts[ch]||0)+1;
  const total = s.length;
  let e=0; for(const c of Object.values(counts)){ const p=c/total; e -= p*Math.log2(p); }
  return e;
}
function isSuspicious(domain){
  const parts = domain.split('.');
  const labels = parts.slice(0,-1);
  const ent = labels.map(l=>entropy(l)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  const len = domain.length;
  return ent>3.5 || len>30;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you tune thresholds to reduce false positives across tenants?
- What data retention and RBAC would you implement for CoreDNS and eBPF telemetry?

## Why This Is Asked
Addresses multi-cloud data governance, tenancy, and compliance through policy-as-code, ensuring data privacy, residency, and auditable CI validation in production-scale ML platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Data masking in logs/telemetry
- Per-tenant data residency and envelope encryption
- Tenant-aware RBAC across clouds
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Kyverno) + CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(logEntry) {
  const redacted = { ...logEntry };
  for (const k of Object.keys(redacted)) {
    if (k.toLowerCase().includes('ssn') || k.toLowerCase().includes('email')) {
      redacted[k] = 'REDACTED';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test fail-closed masking in CI across multiple tenants?
- What metrics would you surface to detect residency violations at runtime?

## Why This Is Asked
A practical beginner-level check for implementing and validating security controls in Kubernetes using policy engines, focusing on least privilege and safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Pod Security Standards Baseline/Restricted
- Policy engines: Kyverno, OPA Gatekeeper
- Privilege escalation controls: runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities
- Testing methods: compliant vs non-compliant pods, namespace scoping
- Change management and rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Conceptual Kyverno policy (JS object for illustration)
const policy = {
  apiVersion: 'kyverno/v1',
  kind: 'ClusterPolicy',
  metadata: { name: 'require-baseline-security' },
  spec: {
    rules: [
      {
        name: 'require-baseline',
        match: { resources: { kinds: ['Pod'] }},
        validate: {
          message: 'Pods must set runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem and avoid hostPath',
          pattern: {
            spec: {
              containers: { any: { securityContext: { runAsNonRoot: true, readOnlyRootFilesystem: true } }},
              hostPath: { absent: {} }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle exceptions for legitimate hostPath usage?
- How would you monitor for policy violations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Designing runtime access control is critical for microservices in a ride-hailing platform. This tests integration of SPIFFE identities, policy as code (OPA), and Envoy ext_authz in a real-world, zero-trust setup.

## Key Concepts
- SPIFFE IDs and mTLS identity
- Open Policy Agent (Rego) for authz decisions
- Envoy ext_authz as a gatekeeper
- Service mesh zero-trust patterns
- CI test harness for policy validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JavaScript test harness exercising OPA policy via REST API
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function test(spiffe_id, expected) {
  const input = { spiffe_id };
  const res = await fetch('http://opa.local/v1/data/authz/allow', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input })
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  console.log(spiffe_id, json.result === expected);
}
(async () => { await test('spiffe://domain.org/finance/payment', true); await test('spiffe://domain.org/unknown', false); })();
```
